```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is the RIP routing daemon (ripd) on router1, which is the
sole transit router connecting the router0 side (Location A / 172.16.x, 192.168.1.0)
to the router2 side (192.168.100.0, 10.50.1.0, and beyond).

The mechanism is as follows: router1's /etc/frr/ripd.conf was reduced to a
zero-byte empty file (confirmed by `ls -la /etc/frr/`). FRR requires a valid
configuration file to launch each daemon; with ripd.conf empty, ripd never
started at container bring-up time. The /etc/frr/daemons file correctly marks
`ripd=yes`, but without configuration ripd is absent from the process table
entirely (confirmed by `ps aux` — only zebra, mgmtd, and staticd are running).

As a result, router1 has no RIP routes in its FIB — only two kernel-connected
/24s (10.16.1.0/24 on eth2 toward router0, and 10.20.1.0/24 on eth3 toward
router2). It is advertising nothing and learning nothing. Both neighbors still
hold stale RIP routes sourced from router1 (router0 sees routes from 10.16.1.2
with ~1:44 left; router2 sees routes from 10.20.1.1 with ~1:08 left), explaining
why reachability is currently partial rather than fully absent — those cached
routes are actively expiring. Once they time out, inter-zone connectivity will
collapse entirely.

Root cause: the ripd.conf on router1 was wiped to an empty file, preventing
ripd from starting and removing router1 from the RIP routing domain.

Corrective fix: Restore /etc/frr/ripd.conf on router1 with a proper RIP
configuration matching its role (e.g., `router rip` / `network 10.0.0.0/8` /
`version 2`), then start ripd via `service frr restart` or
`systemctl restart frr`. Once ripd is running, router1 will re-advertise its
connected links and re-learn remote prefixes, restoring full inter-zone routing.
=== END ===
